#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. The model and the physics {#1-the-model-and-the-physics}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$H = -t \\sum_{\\langle ij\\rangle} \\left(b_i^\\dagger b_j + b_j^\\dagger "
"b_i\\right), \\qquad (b_i^\\dagger)^2 = 0 .$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this model, bosons hop between neighboring sites $\\langle ij\\rangle$ of a square lattice. In principle, a site can hold any number of bosons; however,\n"
"**hard-core** bosons are forbidden to share a site. Thus, every site holds either zero or one boson. In `qiskit-bosons`, that is handled through the\n"
"truncation $d = 2$ (`max_occupation = 1`) during the boson-to-qubit mapping. Each site is then mapped as a two-level system and the model is the spin-1/2 XY model,\n"
"$b_i^\\dagger b_j + \\mathrm{h.c.} = \\tfrac12(X_iX_j + Y_iY_j)$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**How this relates to the Bose-Hubbard model.** Bose-Hubbard adds an on-site repulsion $\\tfrac U2 n_i(n_i-1)$ to the hopping\n"
"term, which penalizes two bosons on one site. Hard-core is the limit $U \\to \\infty$, where double occupation is forbidden\n"
"outright. In the truncated space $n_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, $n_i(n_i-1) = 0$, so the on-site term vanishes whatever $U$ is\n"
"and the Hamiltonian has no interaction parameter at all. Note that this is the opposite of $U = 0$, which is the free\n"
"boson, where a site can hold many bosons.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Why 1D is classically easy and 2D is not.** On an open chain, hard-core bosons are exactly free fermions: the Jordan-Wigner\n"
"transformation turns the spin chain into a quadratic fermion Hamiltonian with no string left over, because particles\n"
"cannot hop past each other. On a grid, a hop around a plaquette exchanges particles, and the sign that the fermions would pick up\n"
"is not matched by the bosons. The cells below check this with `qiskit-fermions`: they build free fermions that hop on the same\n"
"bonds, map them with `jordan_wigner` (qubit $j$ is mode $j$, sites numbered row by row) and compare the spectra with\n"
"the hard-core Hamiltonian in every particle-number sector. The hard-core matrix itself is built with plain numpy, because\n"
"it is a boson model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On the open chain, the two Hamiltonians are the same matrix and the spectra agree to rounding in every sector. On the grids,\n"
"they differ at order one, and the ring with an even particle number fails too, because particles can circle around it. So the\n"
"1D case is classically easy (free fermions are a polynomial problem) and the 2D case is the interesting one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Build the `BosonicCircuit` {#2-build-the-bosoniccircuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A hopping term is Hermitian only together with its conjugate pair, so both stay in one group. Bonds that share no site commute, so an\n"
"**edge coloring** of the square lattice gives groups that commute internally at the bosonic level:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"color 0 / 1: horizontal bonds starting at an even / odd column (blue pair)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"color 2 / 3: vertical bonds starting at an even / odd row (orange and red)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two bonds of the same color never share a site, because the bonds along a line alternate colors. The construction below takes\n"
"only the lattice size $(L_x, L_y)$ as input, and everything after it (circuit, encoding, transpilation) is identical for every\n"
"size. The drawing shows the site numbering ($s = L_x y + x$) and the colors for a $4\\times4$ lattice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/57d33dfd-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run some simple checks of the construction on a few lattices: every color "
"group is Hermitian, and the bonds of a color are site-disjoint (so each "
"group commutes internally).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now construct the circuit. The whole Trotter step is **one** `Evolution` gate over all modes. `BosonicLieTrotter` decides how it splits,\n"
"and one `decompose()` shows the split: one `Evolution` per color and per Trotter step. The circuit mentions neither the\n"
"truncation nor the encoding; those come in when the transpiler preset lowers it to qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/7b527e69-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/c41ffd44-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, construct the transpiler pipeline to lower the `BosonicCircuit` to a `QuantumCircuit`.\n"
"To do so, you need:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`ENCODINGS`: the four `LevelEncoding`s that map a boson mode with $d$ levels to qubits. At $d = 2$, the\n"
"standard-binary and Gray codes are the same code with one qubit per mode (qubit $= $ occupation), while the two unary codes use one qubit per level (two qubits per mode) and differ only in whether they project onto the valid unary states.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`BASIS`: the hardware gate set used for counting (`cz`, `rz`, `sx`, `x`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`to_qubits`: runs the `qiskit-bosons` preset pass manager on a bosonic "
"circuit, with the qubit-side optimization stage switched off so that the "
"circuit stays as synthesized.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`transpile_to_basis`: transpiles a qubit circuit to `BASIS` for counting, onto a chip if a coupling map and a layout are given.\n"
"It calls `decompose(reps=6)` first, because the exact `PauliEvolutionGate` otherwise hides the real gate count.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The qubit circuit of one Trotter step on the $4\\times4$ lattice (Gray code, one qubit per site). Each bond is one exact\n"
"XX+YY rotation, and the bonds of a color sit side by side.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/f880c728-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Compute a Néel quench {#3-compute-a-néel-quench}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can now watch the bosons move under a quench. The dynamics start from a **Néel pattern**: the sites of a checkerboard are alternately\n"
"filled with one boson (black circles in the drawing below) and empty (white circles), so half of the sites are occupied. At\n"
"time zero, the state is a single bit string, which is easy to prepare (one `X` gate per occupied site). It is not an eigenstate of\n"
"the hopping Hamiltonian: every bond of the lattice joins an occupied and an empty site, so every bond can move a boson,\n"
"and the state starts to change right away. Preparing a simple state and then letting it evolve under a Hamiltonian that it\n"
"is not an eigenstate of is called a **quench**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/d8ccb302-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because the lattice is bipartite, a hop always moves a boson to the other sublattice of the checkerboard. This suggests the\n"
"observable to follow, the **staggered density imbalance**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\\mathcal I(t) = \\frac1N \\sum_i (-1)^{x_i+y_i}\\,\\bigl(2\\langle "
"n_i\\rangle(t) - 1\\bigr) = \\frac{2}{N}\\bigl(N_A(t) - N_B(t)\\bigr),$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $N_A$ and $N_B$ are the numbers of bosons on the two sublattices. It measures how many bosons are still on the\n"
"sublattice they started on. It starts at $\\mathcal I = 1$ and, as the bosons spread over the lattice, falls and then\n"
"fluctuates around zero, where neither sublattice is preferred. On hardware, it is cheap to measure, because it only needs\n"
"the occupation of each site, that is, single-qubit $Z$ measurements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The later plots of the observable behavior also show the density of individual sites.\n"
"In one dimension, this quench is a free-fermion problem, but on a square lattice it is not (as discussed in section 1), so the exact evolution of the $4\\times4$ lattice below is the reference. The hard-core Hilbert space is the set of bit strings, and $b_i^\\dagger b_j$ moves a bit. Build $H$ as a sparse matrix with plain numpy and evolve with `expm_multiply`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "First, compute the exact reference.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, check the `InitializeModes` gate, which also confirms that the mode-"
"to-qubit encoding is correct.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now compute the Trotterized curves. A circuit is one first-order Trotter step of length $dt$ (all four colors once), applied\n"
"$T/dt$ times; `trotter_series` returns the site densities at every multiple of $dt$. The cell after it turns the densities of\n"
"the exact and of the Trotterized evolution into the three observables to plot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/dd335add-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here $dt = T/\\text{steps}$ is the length of one Trotter step, so the legend gives both. All three panels show all three step\n"
"sizes. The smaller the step, the closer the circuit follows the exact curve. The coarsest step, $dt = 0.5$, visibly misses it in places, and even $dt = 0.1$ shows a small wobble on the corner site near $t \\approx 1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Quantify the Trotter error {#4-quantify-the-trotter-error}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The only numerical error of the circuit is the **Trotter error between the colors** at the bosonic level. The other two\n"
"places where an error could creep in are exact by construction:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The qubit side.** The Pauli terms of one color commute, because the bonds of a color share no site. Synthesizing a color\n"
"therefore needs no further approximation, and the circuit is the ordered product of the exact color exponentials.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The encoding.** At $d = 2$, the four `LevelEncoding`s are different representations of the same two-level system, so they\n"
"give the same circuit up to cost (section 5).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There is no \"truncation\" error either: the cutoff $d = 2$ *defines* this model (section 1). It is not a numerical\n"
"approximation of something larger.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This section explores `BosonicSuzukiTrotter`. It measures the Trotter error against the exact reference of section 3 at one final time, $T = 2$, versus\n"
"the number of steps, for first and for second order. It uses $T = 2$, not the end of the curves above, on purpose: by\n"
"$t = 3$, the signal has decayed close to its long-time value, so the error there is small and scatters from one step count to\n"
"the next. At $T = 2$, the dynamics are still pronounced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Is the bosonic level needed for this error?** For hard-core bosons, the qubit operator is only $-\\tfrac12(XX + YY)$ per bond,\n"
"so you can also hand it to Qiskit as **one** `PauliEvolutionGate`, without any groups, and let Qiskit's `SuzukiTrotter` product formula split it. A product formula applies the terms in the order it is\n"
"given, so the order decides which terms are exponentiated together. Try three: the terms sorted by edge color (the grouping\n"
"that the bosonic circuit knows), the terms bond by bond, and `preserve_order=False`, which lets Qiskit reorder the terms\n"
"itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/11884bb1-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Left:** the dotted gray lines are the ideal slopes $-1$ and $-2$, and the fitted slope over the last three points of each\n"
"curve is in the legend. The slopes approach the ideal values only once the step is small enough; with few steps, the error is\n"
"outside the asymptotic regime.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Right:** the same errors against the two-qubit depth of the whole circuit, for the same step\n"
"counts. A second-order step has more blocks (14 against 8 layers for one step), but adjacent identical blocks of neighboring\n"
"repetitions merge. For large errors (one or two steps), first order is the cheaper way to reach a given error. Below an error of about $10^{-1}$, second order is cheaper.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Single gates.** The dashed curves are the single `PauliEvolutionGate` with `SuzukiTrotter` of order 1 (green) and 2 (blue). Each\n"
"ordering of the Pauli terms has its own marker and a lighter tint of the color. With the terms sorted by color (crosses), the\n"
"single gate lies exactly on the bosonic curve of the same order: it is the same product formula. In the other two orderings,\n"
"it is a different formula with a different error, which can be several times larger or (at one or two steps) smaller. But the\n"
"**order of magnitude and the scaling match** the bosonic circuit: the first-order curves fall with the ideal slope $-1$ and the\n"
"second-order curves with $-2$, and at 32 steps all three orderings are within a factor of about three of each other. So the Trotter\n"
"error does not decide between the approaches. What differs is the cost (right panel, and the next section): at equal error,\n"
"the squares (medium tint) need a much larger depth, and the triangles (lightest tint) roughly twice the depth of the crosses. At the bosonic level, the\n"
"grouping is part of the operator, so there is no ordering to choose by hand, which makes for a much more ergonomic workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "5. Scale with system size {#5-scale-with-system-size}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This section investigates how cost scales with the lattice size. Since one Trotter step is one $XX+YY$ layer per color, whatever the\n"
"number of sites, the two-qubit **depth should be flat** in the lattice size while the **gate count grows** with the number of\n"
"bonds. The sizes go up to $10\\times12$, the whole Nighthawk chip in the compact codes. The unary codes need two qubits\n"
"per mode, so they stop at the sizes that fit in 120 qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/d4dbf1ea-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The compact code has a flat depth from $3\\times3$ on (every color is a layer of side-by-side $XX+YY$ rotations; the $2\\times2$\n"
"lattice has only two colors, so its depth is half) and a gate count that grows with the number of bonds.\n"
"The unary codes are an order of magnitude more expensive in both. The reason is simple: in the compact codes, a bond maps to one $XX$ and\n"
"one $YY$ term on the two qubits of the bond with equal coefficients, that is, an $XX+YY$ rotation (two `cz`). In the unary codes, each\n"
"mode has two qubits (one per level), and the hop expands into several weight-4 Pauli strings on the four qubits of both\n"
"modes. They all commute, so nothing is saved by commutation. The ratio is a property of the encoding at $d = 2$, and it does\n"
"not change with the lattice size. At $d = 2$, the standard-binary and Gray codes are the same code, and the projected and\n"
"unprojected unary codes emit the same operator, so the compact curve stands for both pairs.\n"
"**Why Trotterize at the bosonic level?** The dashed curves are the same Trotter steps as one `PauliEvolutionGate` over the qubit\n"
"operator (section 4), in green for first order and blue for second order. The solid blue curve is the second-order bosonic circuit,\n"
"the solid green curve the first-order one from above. The single gate with the terms sorted by color (crosses) lies exactly on the\n"
"bosonic curve of its order: a flat depth (8 for first order, 14 for second order) and the minimal gate count (as in the previous section). Qiskit does not find\n"
"that grouping by itself. If the terms are listed bond by bond (squares), the gate count is the same, but the layers are not packed\n"
"and the depth grows with the lattice (at $10\\times12$ it is 84 against 8 for first order and 166 against 14 for second order).\n"
"With `preserve_order=False` (triangles), Qiskit does find commuting terms and the depth stays flat, but it treats the XX and the YY\n"
"term of a bond as separate rotations, so it needs about a third more two-qubit gates and twice the depth. So the bosonic level is\n"
"not magic: it carries the edge coloring in the operator itself, and what you would otherwise have to arrange by hand in the Pauli\n"
"terms (and keep arranged for every lattice) comes with the construction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "6. Transpile for Nighthawk {#6-transpile-for-nighthawk}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`FakeNighthawk` is a $12\\times10$ square lattice with qubit index $10\\,\\text{row} + \\text{col}$. A hard-core lattice is a square\n"
"lattice too, so it sits on the chip as it is: site $(x, y)$ goes to qubit $10y + x$, which embeds an $L_x\\times L_y$ lattice\n"
"natively for $L_x \\le 10$, $L_y \\le 12$ as shown below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/3508477b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, check the native embedding on the **unrouted** circuits. After "
"lowering on the abstract register, every two-qubit gate must sit on a "
"coupled pair under the layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now route onto the chip. Every two-qubit gate is already on a coupler, so the transpiler has no swaps to insert and nothing to\n"
"choose: the routed circuit has the same two-qubit depth and the same two-qubit gate count as the circuit on the abstract\n"
"register, at every size. The cell below transpiles each lattice of the scaling study onto Nighthawk and asserts exactly that.\n"
"The drawing shows the routed circuit for the $4\\times4$ lattice. Each wire is labeled `q_i ↦ p`: virtual qubit `i` of the\n"
"encoded circuit (the lattice site `i`) sits on physical qubit `p` of the device at the start. Only the qubits the circuit acts\n"
"on are drawn.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/hard-core-bosons-"
"dynamics/extracted-outputs/507bdd21-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"7. Beyond hard-core bosons: higher mode truncation {#7-beyond-hard-core-"
"bosons-higher-mode-truncation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Everything so far only considered hard-core bosons, so the on-site term could not act. With $d = 3$, a site can\n"
"hold two bosons, $\\tfrac U2 n(n-1)$ appears as a third group (diagonal), and the hopping matrix elements acquire $\\sqrt2$ factors.\n"
"The next cell builds the Bose-Hubbard step on a 4-site chain and measures its cost the same way, for all four encodings, next to the hard-core chain of the same length.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The physics becomes significantly more complicated and the favorable scaling"
" disappears.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial used hard-core bosons because they make for simple analyses "
"while emphasizing the benefits of the `qiskit-bosons` package and "
"architecture of its transpiler framework. It is set up to investigate real "
"bosonic physics beyond such simple truncation limits, but exploring these "
"circuits and researching their application is now up to you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "8. Takeaways and next steps {#8-takeaways-and-next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The output below recaps the circuit cost of a single Trotter step for the "
"hard-core lattices, and the Trotter error of the small system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"From these numbers, you can draw some high-level takeaways. The first ones are about the framework, which is what you\n"
"carry over to other bosonic models. The later ones are about what the results show for hard-core bosons, the simple test case.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**A framework for bosonic physics.** `qiskit-bosons` separates three things: the model (a `BosonOperator`, grouped here\n"
"by edge color), the circuit (one `Evolution` gate and a Trotter formula that splits it), and the lowering to qubits (the mode\n"
"truncation $d$ and the `LevelEncoding`, chosen when the transpiler preset runs). Changing one of them leaves the other two\n"
"untouched (sections 2 and 7).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Why Trotterize at the bosonic level.** The grouping lives in the operator, so the Trotter circuit comes out with a flat depth\n"
"without any ordering by hand. One `PauliEvolutionGate` over the same qubit operator has the same Trotter error order and\n"
"scaling, and with the terms sorted by color it is the identical circuit. In another order, the depth grows with the lattice\n"
"(84 against 8 for one first-order step at $10\\times12$) or the gate count is about a third higher (sections 4 and 5).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Accuracy.** The only numerical error is the Trotter error between the colors, and it falls with the number of steps as\n"
"expected for first and second order (section 4). The qubit side and the encoding add nothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The encoding is a cost choice.** At $d = 2$, all four encodings give the same physics. The compact codes need half the\n"
"qubits and are about 20 times cheaper in depth and gates than the unary ones (section 5).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Hardware.** With the natural embedding of the lattice, every gate already sits on a Nighthawk coupler, so the chip runs the\n"
"circuit with no routing overhead at any size up to the whole chip (section 6).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* **Hard-core bosons are the entry point, not the goal.** They are the $U\\to\\infty$ limit of Bose-Hubbard, with the XY model as\n"
"  a spin-1/2 equivalent. They are exactly free fermions on an open chain but not on a square lattice, which makes 2D the\n"
"  interesting case (section 1). Raising the truncation to $d = 3$ brings the on-site interaction back and the cost jumps (a\n"
"  4-site chain goes from a two-qubit depth of 4 to 95 in the Gray code, section 7). That is where the exploration of bosonic\n"
"  physics begins, and the same framework carries it.\n"
"**Caveats.** All depths are for a noise-free fake backend (`FakeNighthawk`) and one fixed basis (`cz, rz, sx, x`, optimization\n"
"level 3); they compare structure, not fidelity. The routed numbers equal the abstract ones only because the natural embedding\n"
"puts every gate on a coupler. The Néel quench is one initial state; the Trotter error depends on the state and on $T$. Nothing\n"
"here runs on real hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Next steps.** Hard-core bosons are a simple, checkable model to learn the framework on. From here, the same building\n"
"blocks apply to the models that are hard to treat classically:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Larger cutoffs $d$ (soft-core bosons, the full Bose-Hubbard model), where the choice of encoding and of Trotter formula\n"
"matters much more than here (section 7)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Other Hamiltonians, such as bosons with a different interaction or a "
"different coupling to their environment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Other lattices, such as triangular or honeycomb, which need other edge "
"colorings and other hardware embeddings\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Correlation functions as observables, such as $\\langle X_iX_j + "
"Y_iY_j\\rangle$ and density-density correlators\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mixed models (bosons coupled to fermions or to a bath)\n"
msgstr ""

